{
  "configuration_id": "config_030",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_018 (multi-profile, PCA, ResNet50, avgpool, concat) is the clear best at 0.619 acc, and RNA is the dominant modality (0.485 alone vs 0.208 for image alone). However, all experiments so far use PCA—a simple linear dimensionality reduction—as the RNA encoder. The RNA encoder is the single most impactful untested design variable given RNA's dominance. Question: Does a learned, nonlinear RNA embedding (scvi_n200, designed for single-cell stochastic structure) outperform PCA in the best-performing multimodal multi-profile setup? Why this is the best next experiment: config_030 changes only the RNA encoder (PCA → scvi_n200) while holding all other settings at their empirically best values (multiprofile, avgpool, concat, ResNet50). This clean single-variable test directly quantifies the value of a learned RNA representation. If scvi improves substantially, it opens a high-value direction; if it does not, we can confidently rule out RNA encoder upgrades and pivot to the image encoder (ViT-L) as the next lever. No other untested configuration isolates as important an unexplored axis with as high expected information gain under the limited budget."
}

